 @Unordinary-lg4yt  I have an ADA 6000 at work on 4k monitors and an XTX at home on 1440p monitors, so I go from one to the other 5 times a week. I can perfectly well afford Nvidia's flagships. Not a problem at all. To me it's a bout principle. Nvidia marketing will have you belive that zooming 3x on a still-shot and pointing to a few minute details is worth what they are asking. And it's blatantly false. You stop noticing SO fast when you actually get immersed in the game.

Point in case: My laptop is a Ryzen 7840U which only has a little more graphics power than a Steam Deck. When I go from my XTX (1440p Native, Ultra at ~120FPS) to my laptop (1080p FSR Performance, Low at 60FPS locked) in Eternal Strands I notice for about 10 minutes before my brain adjusts. And that's walking 12 steps from my home office to my couch.

I'm not "bitter" that people buy Nvidia. I just think they have FOMO syndrome so bad it's not even funny.... and half of them, to me, sound like they are defending the drug dealer who got them hooked in the first place.

 @RobertZ1973   Context? Think little monkey, the 3080 definitely should've had more vram, but for the price the 3070 obliterates the 2080ti, even the 2080super obliterates the 2080ti. 
In terms of generational uplift in mid range cards from nvidia, 30 series was the last one price wise, the 5080 will age like milk too, and at a MUCH worse price than the 3070. 
$500 isn't nothing, and I own a $5000 DAC, just because one can take out a loan to afford 2 years of crappy rt performance, doesn't make it worth it buddy, flagships always get trounced by next gens mid range, less so now, but it's still the case. The 2080ti will age worse than the 4090 considering the 1080ti was so much better value too, the 4090 wins in the long run simply by virtue of the 50 series sucking. As I said if you want to be an early adopter of flagships, which I doubt a baby who has  to cope with defending an overpriced gen 1 RT flagship for this long is, then you can do that, but $500 vs $1000 is not negotiable, the 3070 is better value even with 8gb. A used 2080ti if you can get it at a good price is still nice though, but it's gen 1 RT cores are useless, and its release MSRP was an embarrassment compared to the 1080ti.

